1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,141
Picture the demo that got this feature greenlit.

2
00:00:03,141 --> 00:00:07,851
Someone picked the prompt, tried it a few times first, and the room nodded along.

3
00:00:07,851 --> 00:00:12,735
Two weeks into production, the same feature meets a message nobody rehearsed, and it

4
00:00:12,735 --> 00:00:16,979
answers just as confidently as it did in the demo, except now it's wrong.

5
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:19,712
That gap is not a bug that crept in afterwards.

6
00:00:19,712 --> 00:00:24,480
It's the mechanism, doing exactly what it always does, just facing an input nobody

7
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,817
chose for it this time.

8
00:00:25,817 --> 00:00:30,411
Strip away the marketing and the mystique, and here's what's actually happening

9
00:00:30,411 --> 00:00:35,237
inside every one of these systems: given everything so far, predict the single most

10
00:00:35,237 --> 00:00:36,225
likely next word.

11
00:00:36,225 --> 00:00:36,632
Add it.

12
00:00:36,632 --> 00:00:37,330
Do it again.

13
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:38,900
That's the whole mechanism.

14
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:40,993
Not reasoning in the way you reason.

15
00:00:40,993 --> 00:00:44,598
Not looking things up the way a search engine looks things up.

16
00:00:44,598 --> 00:00:47,796
Pattern completion, one word at a time, extremely fast.

17
00:00:47,796 --> 00:00:50,063
So what follows from that one sentence?

18
00:00:50,063 --> 00:00:54,831
I want to spend this lecture on four things, because none of them are obvious from

19
00:00:54,831 --> 00:00:59,366
using the product, and every one of them will eventually cost you if you don't

20
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:00,413
already know them.

21
00:01:00,413 --> 00:01:02,041
None of this is a criticism.

22
00:01:02,041 --> 00:01:06,809
It's just what the mechanism is, and a PM who knows the mechanism makes different,

23
00:01:06,809 --> 00:01:09,949
better decisions than one working from the demo alone.

24
00:01:10,499 --> 00:01:15,429
Here's that sentence as a loop, because it really is just this, run thousands of

25
00:01:15,429 --> 00:01:16,477
times per answer.

26
00:01:16,477 --> 00:01:17,895
Start with your prompt.

27
00:01:17,895 --> 00:01:22,949
Predict one word: the single most statistically likely next word, given everything

28
00:01:22,949 --> 00:01:23,380
so far.

29
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:23,997
Append it.

30
00:01:23,997 --> 00:01:28,003
Now that word is part of "everything so far," so go around again.

31
00:01:28,003 --> 00:01:29,297
Predict the next one.

32
00:01:29,297 --> 00:01:29,914
Append it.

33
00:01:29,914 --> 00:01:31,886
Repeat until the answer is done.

34
00:01:31,886 --> 00:01:36,878
There's no separate step where it "thinks it through" first and writes the answer

35
00:01:36,878 --> 00:01:37,556
down after.

36
00:01:37,556 --> 00:01:42,426
The writing is the thinking, and once a word is generated, the model is already

37
00:01:42,426 --> 00:01:47,356
partly committed to whatever comes next, because that word is now sitting in the

38
00:01:47,356 --> 00:01:49,575
context feeding the next prediction.

39
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:53,555
Watch the mechanism produce a genuinely dangerous sentence.

40
00:01:53,555 --> 00:01:58,322
Ask a model, with nothing in the prompt about your actual policy, what your refund

41
00:01:58,322 --> 00:01:58,903
policy is.

42
00:01:58,903 --> 00:02:03,670
It doesn't say "I don't have that information." It predicts the most statistically

43
00:02:03,670 --> 00:02:08,437
likely continuation of a sentence that starts with "our refund policy is," because

44
00:02:08,437 --> 00:02:13,146
somewhere in an ocean of training text, refund policies overwhelmingly sound like

45
00:02:13,146 --> 00:02:13,437
that.

46
00:02:13,437 --> 00:02:16,983
Thirty days, full refund, is a genuinely common policy shape.

47
00:02:16,983 --> 00:02:19,773
It might even be close to yours, by coincidence.

48
00:02:19,773 --> 00:02:21,692
It is not looking up your policy.

49
00:02:21,692 --> 00:02:26,517
It's completing a plausible sentence, with exactly the same confident tone it would

50
00:02:26,517 --> 00:02:28,435
use if it happened to be correct.

51
00:02:28,435 --> 00:02:32,446
Picture a support agent trusting that sentence for one real customer.

52
00:02:32,446 --> 00:02:35,353
Nothing about the delivery would have warned them.

53
00:02:35,353 --> 00:02:40,062
That's the entire danger in one exchange: not that it's wrong, but that wrong and

54
00:02:40,062 --> 00:02:41,341
right sound identical.

55
00:02:41,891 --> 00:02:45,924
Four consequences, and you just watched two of them happen at once.

56
00:02:45,924 --> 00:02:50,800
It completes patterns, not intentions, so an ambiguous question gets a confident,

57
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:55,615
plausible-sounding answer instead of the clarifying question a person would ask.

58
00:02:55,615 --> 00:03:00,612
It has no built-in confidence signal separate from fluency, so a wrong answer and a

59
00:03:00,612 --> 00:03:03,140
right one arrive in exactly the same tone.

60
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:05,006
You cannot hear the difference.

61
00:03:05,006 --> 00:03:10,062
It only knows what's in its training data plus whatever you put in the prompt, which

62
00:03:10,062 --> 00:03:15,059
is precisely why it invented your refund policy instead of admitting it didn't have

63
00:03:15,059 --> 00:03:15,299
one.

64
00:03:15,299 --> 00:03:19,573
And it reflects the statistical centre of everything it was trained on.

65
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:21,078
Common patterns dominate.

66
00:03:21,078 --> 00:03:25,352
A rare, correct, specialist answer competes against a much more common,

67
00:03:25,352 --> 00:03:29,024
plausible-sounding, wrong one, and the common one often wins.

68
00:03:29,024 --> 00:03:30,288
Hold onto that table.

69
00:03:30,288 --> 00:03:35,344
Nearly every failure mode in the rest of this course is one of these four, wearing a

70
00:03:35,344 --> 00:03:36,428
different costume.

71
00:03:36,978 --> 00:03:39,232
That's the term for the whole mechanism.

72
00:03:39,232 --> 00:03:43,909
Not magic, not reasoning in the way the word usually means: pattern completion, one

73
00:03:43,909 --> 00:03:48,361
token at a time, at a speed and fluency that makes it feel like something else.

74
00:03:48,361 --> 00:03:52,475
The exercise takes thirty seconds, and it's the one that actually sticks.

75
00:03:52,475 --> 00:03:56,870
Ask any model something only your company could know, with zero context given.

76
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:01,604
Whatever it says back, confidently, is the mechanism working exactly as designed, on

77
00:04:01,604 --> 00:04:03,858
a question it had no business answering.

78
00:04:04,408 --> 00:04:08,991
Say the honest caveat, because overclaiming in the other direction is just as

79
00:04:08,991 --> 00:04:09,645
misleading.

80
00:04:09,645 --> 00:04:14,407
What you just watched is the base mechanism, not the entire product you actually

81
00:04:14,407 --> 00:04:14,645
use.

82
00:04:14,645 --> 00:04:19,584
Real systems wrap it in guardrails, give it tools to look things up, and connect it

83
00:04:19,584 --> 00:04:24,584
to your actual documents, which is most of what the RAG and agents sections later in

84
00:04:24,584 --> 00:04:25,893
this course are about.

85
00:04:25,893 --> 00:04:29,345
But every one of those additions sits on top of this loop.

86
00:04:29,345 --> 00:04:33,571
They don't replace next-token prediction, they constrain and inform it.

87
00:04:33,571 --> 00:04:38,570
Understanding the loop is what lets you tell the difference between a guardrail that

88
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:41,010
actually works and one that's decoration.

89
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:43,249
One thing before you move on.

90
00:04:43,249 --> 00:04:47,910
Ask a model one real question only your company could answer, with nothing about

91
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:49,483
your company in the prompt.

92
00:04:49,483 --> 00:04:54,377
Then notice, specifically, whether it hedged, or answered with the same confidence a

93
00:04:54,377 --> 00:04:55,950
correct answer would carry.

94
00:04:55,950 --> 00:05:00,610
Next lecture takes the other half of that loop seriously: what's actually inside

95
00:05:00,610 --> 00:05:05,388
"everything written so far," how big that space is, and exactly where your feature

96
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:08,184
quietly breaks once real conversations get long.
